## ABSTRACT

This paper presents a rigorous theoretical framework for the Photonically-Coupled Magnetostructural Dexterous Hand Architecture (PCMDHA) — a field-organised embodiment paradigm specifically engineered for ultra-light humanoid fingers and hands. Departing entirely from tendon-driven transmissions, cable routing, and rigid geared joints that currently dominate robotic hand design, the PCMDHA achieves structural coherence, multi-DOF articulation, distributed proprioception, and compliant grasping through coordinated electromagnetic fields embedded within hollow composite phalanx lattices, Halbach magnetic inter-phalanx couplings, distributed micro-coil actuators, and differential polymer optical waveguide networks. The hand is formalised as a seven-tuple over a constrained admissible state space encoding all five finger chains, the metacarpal base, and the wrist interface. Quasi-static well-posedness is proved via Korn's inequality and Lax-Milgram. Under linearised dissipative magnetoelastic assumptions the coupled hand system admits exponential stability; a constructive damping coefficient is derived explicitly as a function of coil geometry and Halbach field strength. A stiffness-separation theorem guarantees full structural observability of all finger joint angles simultaneously from differential photonic measurements, with a provably diagonal sensing Jacobian. Analytical models for phalanx-scale holding force (310 N at contact, 185 N at 1 mm gap), actuator force-to-mass ratio (80–180 N kg⁻¹), photonic strain resolution (0.12 dB deg⁻¹), inter-phalanx magnetic crosstalk (< 2% force perturbation at 40 mm spacing), and thermal equilibrium at continuous operation are derived incorporating realistic material parameters, orthotropic composite Brazier-shell buckling with NASA knockdown factor κ = 0.65, Halbach field-enhancement curves, and stroke-to-joint kinematic conversion for finger flexion-extension and abduction-adduction. A complete biomechanical correspondence map between the 27 degrees of freedom of the human hand and the PCMDHA kinematic graph is established. The architecture is benchmarked transparently against state-of-the-art tendon-driven and linkage-based robotic hands. A tiered multiphysics validation framework is specified. The work is confined to physical embodiment; AI cognition, grasp planning, and high-level control are explicitly excluded.

**Keywords:** dexterous robotic hand, magnetostructural phalanx, Halbach coupling, distributed electromagnetic actuation, polymer optical waveguide sensing, field-organised morphology, Lyapunov stability, structural observability, composite buckling, finger kinematics.

