## 2. BIOMECHANICAL FOUNDATION AND DOF CORRESPONDENCE

### 2.1 Human Hand Kinematic Graph

The human hand is modelled as a rooted directed kinematic graph G_hand = (V, E) where V is the set of rigid bodies (bones) and E is the set of joints. The root is the wrist (radiocarpal joint). The graph has the following structure:

**Wrist level:** 2 DOF — flexion-extension (FE), radial-ulnar deviation (RUD).

**Metacarpal level:** Fingers II–V have metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints with 2 DOF each (FE + abduction-adduction AB). Thumb (digit I) has a carpometacarpal (CMC) joint with 2 DOF (opposition, flexion) and an MCP with 1 DOF.

**Proximal level:** Proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints for fingers II–V: 1 DOF each (FE only). Thumb interphalangeal (IP): 1 DOF.

**Distal level:** Distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints for fingers II–V: 1 DOF each (FE only, kinematically coupled to PIP in natural motion).

Total DOF count:

$$N_{\text{DOF,hand}} = \underbrace{2}_{\text{wrist}} + \underbrace{4 \times 2}_{\text{MCP II–V}} + \underbrace{2 + 1}_{\text{thumb CMC+MCP}} + \underbrace{4 \times 1}_{\text{PIP}} + \underbrace{1}_{\text{thumb IP}} + \underbrace{4 \times 1}_{\text{DIP}} = 2 + 8 + 3 + 4 + 1 + 4 = 27$$

### 2.2 PCMDHA Kinematic Graph

**Definition 2.1 (PCMDHA Kinematic Graph).** The PCMDHA kinematic graph is the five-tuple

$$\mathcal{G}_{\text{PCMDHA}} = (V_P, E_P, \boldsymbol{\tau}, \boldsymbol{\kappa}, \boldsymbol{\psi})$$

where V_P = {M_i} is the set of hollow composite phalanx modules; E_P is the set of Halbach magnetic inter-phalanx couplings (joints); τ: E_P → {FE, AB, OP} assigns a joint type to each coupling; κ: E_P → ℝ_+ assigns an angular stiffness K_φ; and ψ: E_P → ℝ_+ assigns an actuator stroke s_max.

