## 4. PHALANX STRUCTURAL ARCHITECTURE

### 4.1 Geometric Model at Finger Scale

Each phalanx module $M_i$ is a hollow cylinder of length $L_i$, outer radius $R_{\text{outer}}$, inner radius $R_{\text{inner}}$. The hollowness ratio is

$$\eta = \left(\frac{R_{\text{inner}}}{R_{\text{outer}}}\right)^2$$

The wall is a symmetric $[0°/90°]_s$ cross-ply CFRP laminate with fibre volume fraction $V_f = 0.6$. Homogenised orthotropic properties via classical lamination theory:

$$E_L = 135 \text{ GPa},\quad E_T = 10 \text{ GPa},\quad G_{LT} = 5 \text{ GPa},\quad \nu_{LT} = 0.25,\quad \rho_c = 1570 \text{ kg m}^{-3}$$

The stiffness tensor in Voigt notation:

$$C_{11} = \frac{E_L}{1 - \nu_{LT}\nu_{TL}},\quad C_{22} = C_{33} = \frac{E_T}{1 - \nu_{LT}\nu_{TL}},\quad C_{12} = \frac{\nu_{LT} E_T}{1 - \nu_{LT}\nu_{TL}}$$

$$C_{44} = G_{LT},\quad C_{55} = C_{66} = G_{TT},\quad \nu_{TL} = \nu_{LT} \frac{E_T}{E_L} = 0.0185$$

Internal lattice: $N_{\text{strut}}$ slender CFRP rods crossing the hollow core at angles $\theta_k \in [25°, 60°]$ to the phalanx axis, resisting torsional and bending loads.

### 4.2 Mass Decomposition

$$m_i = m_{\text{shell}} + m_{\text{struts}} + m_{\text{Halbach}} + m_{\text{coils}} + m_{\text{wg}}$$

with explicit terms:

$$m_{\text{shell}} = \rho_c \pi (R_{\text{outer}}^2 - R_{\text{inner}}^2) L_i$$

$$m_{\text{struts}} = N_{\text{strut}} \rho_c A_{\text{strut}} \frac{2R_{\text{inner}}}{\sin\theta_k}$$

$$m_{\text{Halbach}} = 2 \rho_{\text{mag}} \pi (R_m^2 - R_{m,\text{in}}^2) h_m,\quad \rho_{\text{mag}} = 7500 \text{ kg m}^{-3}$$

$$m_{\text{coils}} = N_{\text{coils}} \rho_{\text{Cu}} N_{\text{turns}} (2w + 2l) A_{\text{wire}}$$

$$m_{\text{wg}} = 2 N_{\text{pairs}} \rho_{\text{PDMS}} \cdot \frac{\pi d_{\text{core}}^2}{4} \cdot L_{\text{wg}}$$

### 4.3 Bending Mechanics and Orthotropic Brazier Buckling

Under a tip load $F_{\text{tip}}$ the maximum bending stress at the fixed end of phalanx $M_i$ is

$$\sigma_{\max} = \frac{M_{\max} R_{\text{outer}}}{I} = \frac{32 F_{\text{tip}} L_i}{\pi D_{\text{outer}}^3 (1 - \eta^2)}$$

The Tsai-Wu failure criterion for the $[0°/90°]_s$ laminate is:

$$F_1 \sigma_1 + F_{11} \sigma_1^2 + F_2 \sigma_2 + F_{22} \sigma_2^2 + 2F_{12}\sigma_1\sigma_2 \leq 1$$

with $F_1 = 1/X_T - 1/X_C$, $F_{11} = 1/(X_T X_C)$, $X_T = 2550$ MPa, $X_C = 1500$ MPa.

For orthotropic composite Brazier buckling, the critical bending moment adopts the semi-empirical form (Tatting et al., 1996):

$$M_{\text{cr}} = \kappa \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{9} \frac{\pi E_{\text{hoop}} t_c^2 R_{\text{outer}}}{\sqrt{3(1 - \nu_{LT}\nu_{TL})}}$$

where $E_{\text{hoop}} \approx \frac{1}{2}(E_T + E_L) = 72.5$ GPa and knockdown factor $\kappa = 0.65$. The minimum wall thickness:

$$t_{\text{Brazier}} = \left( \frac{9\sqrt{3(1 - \nu_{LT}\nu_{TL})}\, M}{2\sqrt{2}\,\kappa\,\pi E_{\text{hoop}} R_{\text{outer}}} \right)^{1/2}$$

**Theorem 4.1 (Optimal Hollowness for Finger Phalanges).**
The mass-optimal hollowness ratio $\eta^*$ for a bending-critical phalanx module subject to stress and composite Brazier buckling constraints is

$$\eta^* = \max\left\{0,\; 1 - \frac{2 t_{\min}}{D_{\text{outer}}}\right\},\quad t_{\min} = \max(t_{\text{stress}},\; t_{\text{Brazier}})$$

where $t_{\text{stress}}$ satisfies $\frac{32 F_{\text{tip}} L_i}{\pi D_{\text{outer}}^3 (1 - (1 - 2t_{\text{stress}}/D_{\text{outer}})^2)^2} = \sigma_y / \text{SF}$.

**Proof.** Module mass is $m_i \propto \pi (R_{\text{outer}}^2 - R_{\text{inner}}^2) L_i = \pi D_{\text{outer}} t_c L_i$ (to leading order in $t_c \ll D_{\text{outer}}$). Since $m_i$ is monotonically increasing in $t_c$, the optimal design minimises $t_c$ subject to two inequality constraints: (a) the bending stress constraint $\sigma_{\max}(t_c) \leq \sigma_y/\text{SF}$ and (b) the Brazier buckling constraint $M \leq M_{\text{cr}}(t_c)$. Both constraints define lower bounds on $t_c$: from (a) one obtains $t_{\text{stress}}$, from (b) one obtains $t_{\text{Brazier}}$. The binding constraint is $t_{\min} = \max(t_{\text{stress}}, t_{\text{Brazier}})$. Setting $t_c = t_{\min}$ yields $\eta^*$. Since $\eta^* \in [0,1)$ by construction and both constraints are feasible for physically reasonable loads, the maximum is well-defined. $\square$

**Numerical computation per finger segment.** Setting $F_{\text{tip}} = 30$ N (representative pinch force on a proximal phalanx), $L_i = 45$ mm, $D_{\text{outer}} = 14$ mm, $\kappa = 0.65$:

$$t_{\text{Brazier}} = \left(\frac{9\sqrt{3(1 - 0.25 \times 0.0185)} \times 30 \times 0.045}{2\sqrt{2} \times 0.65 \times \pi \times 72.5 \times 10^9 \times 0.007}\right)^{1/2} \approx 0.41 \text{ mm}$$

$$t_{\text{stress}} = \frac{32 \times 30 \times 0.045}{\pi \times (0.014)^3 \times 2550 \times 10^6 / 3} \approx 0.22 \text{ mm}$$

Hence $t_{\min} = 0.41$ mm (buckling controls), $\eta^* = 1 - 2 \times 0.41/14 \approx 0.941$.

