### 4.4 Internal Strut Stability

**Theorem 4.2 (Strut Buckling for Finger Phalanges).**
Each internal CFRP strut of radius $r_s$ and length $L_{\text{strut}} = 2R_{\text{inner}}/\sin\theta_k$ resists local buckling provided

$$N_{\text{strut}} \geq \frac{4 P_{\text{distributed}} L_{\text{strut}}^2}{\pi^3 E_L r_s^4}$$

**Proof.** Each strut is modelled as a pin-ended Euler column. Critical load: $P_{\text{cr}} = \pi^2 E_L I_s / L_{\text{strut}}^2$ with $I_s = \pi r_s^4/4$. Requiring $N_{\text{strut}} P_{\text{cr}} \geq P_{\text{distributed}}$ yields the inequality after substitution. $\square$

For the proximal phalanx module with $r_s = 0.3$ mm, $\theta_k = 45°$, $L_{\text{strut}} = 2 \times 6.5 / \sin 45° \approx 18.4$ mm, $P_{\text{distributed}} = 5$ N: $N_{\text{strut}} \geq 4$.

### 4.5 First Bending Natural Frequency

The first bending natural frequency of a cantilever phalanx module is

$$\omega_1 = \frac{(1.875)^2}{L_i^2} \sqrt{\frac{E_L I}{\rho_c A}},\quad f_1 = \frac{\omega_1}{2\pi}$$

where $I = \frac{\pi}{4}(R_{\text{outer}}^4 - R_{\text{inner}}^4)$ and $A = \pi(R_{\text{outer}}^2 - R_{\text{inner}}^2)$.

For the proximal phalanx ($L_i = 45$ mm, $D_{\text{outer}} = 14$ mm, $\eta = 0.941$): $f_1 \approx 680$ Hz, far above all finger manipulation bandwidths (< 20 Hz), justifying quasi-static structural assumptions throughout.

