## 5. INTER-PHALANX MAGNETIC COUPLING VIA HALBACH ARRAYS

### 5.1 Halbach Ring Geometry and Field Enhancement at Finger Scale

Each inter-phalanx joint uses two coaxial Halbach rings, each with $N_{\text{seg}} \geq 8$ NdFeB segments (grade N52SH, $B_r = 1.45$ T, $\mu_r = 1.05$), thickness $h_m$, median radius $R_m = (R_{\text{outer}} + R_{\text{inner}})/2$.

The peak gap field is

$$B_{\text{gap}}(g) = B_r \kappa_H(g) f_{\text{fill}}$$

where $f_{\text{fill}} \approx 0.95$ and the Halbach enhancement factor retaining the fundamental harmonic is

$$\kappa_H(g) = \frac{4}{\pi} \sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{N_{\text{seg}}}\right) \frac{e^{-g/R_m}}{1 - e^{-2h_m/R_m}} \cosh\!\left(\frac{g}{2R_m}\right)$$

For finger-scale rings ($N_{\text{seg}} = 12$, $h_m = 2$ mm, $R_m = 5$ mm):

$$\kappa_H(0) \approx 1.38,\quad \kappa_H(1 \text{ mm}) \approx 1.17,\quad \kappa_H(2 \text{ mm}) \approx 0.91$$

### 5.2 Inter-Phalanx Holding Force and Angular Stiffness

The axial holding force with demagnetisation correction:

$$F_{\text{hold}}(g) = \frac{B_{\text{gap}}(g)^2 A_{\text{eff}}}{2\mu_0} C_{\text{gap}}(g)$$

$$C_{\text{gap}}(g) = 1 - \frac{\mu_r - 1}{\mu_r} \frac{g}{g + h_m} \approx 1 - \frac{0.05}{1.05} \frac{g}{g + h_m}$$

$A_{\text{eff}} = \pi(R_m^2 - R_{m,\text{in}}^2)$ is the effective pole face area.

For the proximal phalanx rings ($R_m = 5$ mm, $R_{m,\text{in}} = 3$ mm, $h_m = 2$ mm): $A_{\text{eff}} = \pi(25 - 9) \times 10^{-6} \approx 50.3 \text{ mm}^2$.

$$B_{\text{gap}}(0) = 1.45 \times 1.38 \times 0.95 \approx 1.90 \text{ T}$$

$$F_{\text{hold}}(0) = \frac{(1.90)^2 \times 50.3 \times 10^{-6}}{2 \times 4\pi \times 10^{-7}} \approx 144 \text{ N}$$

$$F_{\text{hold}}(1 \text{ mm}) \approx 87 \text{ N}$$

Angular misalignment $\phi$ about the joint axis generates restoring torque:

$$T_{\text{restore}}(\phi) \approx -K_\phi \phi,\quad K_\phi \approx \frac{F_{\text{hold}}(g) R_{\text{eff}}}{2},\quad R_{\text{eff}} \approx R_m$$

For the proximal phalanx joint at $g = 1$ mm: $K_\phi \approx 87 \times 0.005 / 2 \approx 0.22 \text{ N·m rad}^{-1}$.

The interface unlocks at a critical overload angle $\phi_c$, providing inherent mechanical overload protection — a critical safety feature for human-robot interaction.

