### 5.3 Inter-Finger Magnetic Crosstalk Analysis

In the dense finger arrangement of the human hand, adjacent metacarpal modules are separated by approximately $d = 20$–$25$ mm. The stray field of a Halbach ring is approximated by a magnetic dipole of moment

$$\mathbf{m} = \frac{B_r V_{\text{mag}}}{\mu_0} \hat{\mathbf{z}},\quad V_{\text{mag}} = \pi(R_m^2 - R_{m,\text{in}}^2) h_m N_{\text{seg}}$$

The axial stray field at distance $d$:

$$B_{\text{stray}} \approx \frac{\mu_0}{2\pi} \frac{|\mathbf{m}|}{d^3}$$

For metacarpal rings ($V_{\text{mag}} \approx 0.8$ cm³, $d = 22$ mm):

$$|\mathbf{m}| = \frac{1.45 \times 0.8 \times 10^{-6}}{4\pi \times 10^{-7}} \approx 924 \text{ A·m}^2$$

$$B_{\text{stray}} \approx \frac{4\pi \times 10^{-7}}{2\pi} \frac{924}{(0.022)^3} \approx 17.1 \text{ mT}$$

This is non-negligible at inter-metacarpal spacing. The force perturbation on an adjacent ring:

$$\Delta F \approx \frac{B_{\text{stray}} B_{\text{gap}} A_{\text{eff}}}{\mu_0} \approx \frac{0.017 \times 1.6 \times 50 \times 10^{-6}}{4\pi \times 10^{-7}} \approx 1.1 \text{ N}$$

relative to $F_{\text{hold}} \approx 195$ N, this is a 0.56% perturbation — within the 3.9% threshold. However, for distal joints at closer spacing ($d = 12$ mm, e.g. between neighbouring DIP joints during a fist posture), a 0.05 mm mu-metal foil liner reduces stray field by a factor > 10, bringing crosstalk below 0.1%.

