## 6. DISTRIBUTED ELECTROMAGNETIC ACTUATION AND FINGER JOINT KINEMATICS

### 6.1 Lorentz Force Model

Each finger joint actuator is a rectangular micro-coil of $N$ turns, dimensions $w \times l$, immersed in the Halbach-generated field $B_0 = B_{\text{gap}}(g_{\text{act}})$. The Lorentz force on a single turn carrying current $I$:

$$\mathbf{F}_L = I \oint (d\mathbf{l} \times \mathbf{B})$$

For a rectangular coil with two active sides of length $w$ in the field:

$$F_L = 2NIwB_0$$

The force constant is $k_F = 2NwB_0$ [N/A]. Resistance of the coil:

$$R_c = \rho_{\text{Cu}} \frac{N(2w + 2l)}{A_{\text{wire}}}$$

Continuous current limit from thermal constraint ($T_{\text{coil}} \leq 80°$C for N52 magnets):

$$I_{\text{cont}} = \sqrt{\frac{h_c A_{\text{surf}} \Delta T_{\max}}{R_c}}$$

For the proximal phalanx coil ($N = 80$ turns, $w = 5$ mm, $l = 4$ mm, $B_0 = 0.8$ T, $A_{\text{wire}} = 0.05$ mm²): $k_F = 2 \times 80 \times 0.005 \times 0.8 = 0.64$ N/A, $R_c \approx 3.6$ Ω, $F_L(1\text{ A}) = 0.64$ N.

### 6.2 Stroke-to-Joint-Angle Kinematics for Finger Flexion-Extension

**Definition 6.1 (Stroke-to-Angle Map).** For a linear Lorentz actuator with stroke $s$ placed at moment arm $r$ from the joint rotation axis:

$$\Delta q = \frac{s}{r} \quad [\text{rad}]$$

**Theorem 6.1 (Push-Pull DOF Range).** A push-pull configuration with two opposed coil modules, each with stroke $s_{\max}$, achieves a total joint angle range of

$$\Delta q_{\text{total}} = \frac{2 s_{\max}}{r}$$

**Proof.** Coil (+) produces flexion stroke $+s_{\max}$, coil (−) produces extension stroke $+s_{\max}$. The joint traverses $+s_{\max}/r$ to $-s_{\max}/r$ relative to equilibrium, giving total range $2s_{\max}/r$. $\square$

For the PIP joint ($s_{\max} = 3$ mm, $r = 7$ mm): $\Delta q_{\text{total}} = 6/7 \approx 0.857$ rad $\approx 49°$, adequately covering the anatomical PIP range of $0°$–$100°$ with three distributed actuator stages.

Peak joint torque per actuator stage: $T_{\text{peak}} = F_L \cdot r = 0.64 \times 0.007 = 4.5$ mN·m per coil. With a circumferential array of 6 coils sharing the joint load: $T_{\text{joint}} \approx 27$ mN·m.

