## 9. STRUCTURAL OBSERVABILITY VIA DIFFERENTIAL PHOTONIC PAIRS

### 9.1 Stiffness-Separation Condition for Fingers

For the photonic Jacobian to remain diagonal across all 27 DOF, each module must be significantly stiffer than its coupling joints so that waveguide bending between joints is dominated by joint rotation rather than module elastic deformation.

**Definition 9.1 (Stiffness Separation Ratio).**

$$R_{\text{stiff}} = \frac{k_m}{k_j L_i}$$

where $k_m = 3E_L I / L_i^3$ is the cantilever bending stiffness of the module and $k_j = K_\phi / L_i$ is the effective joint stiffness normalised by module length.

**Theorem 9.1 (Stiffness Separation for All Finger Modules).**
For all 20 phalanx modules in the PCMDHA hand, $R_{\text{stiff}} \geq 480$, comfortably exceeding the required threshold of 20.

**Proof.** For the proximal phalanx ($L_i = 45$ mm, $D_{\text{outer}} = 14$ mm, $\eta = 0.941$):

$$I = \frac{\pi}{4}\left(R_{\text{outer}}^4 - R_{\text{inner}}^4\right) = \frac{\pi}{4}\left(7^4 - 6.79^4\right) \times 10^{-12} \approx 1.2 \times 10^{-11} \text{ m}^4$$

$$k_m = \frac{3 \times 135 \times 10^9 \times 1.2 \times 10^{-11}}{(0.045)^3} \approx 5.3 \times 10^4 \text{ N m}^{-1}$$

$$k_j = \frac{K_\phi}{L_i} = \frac{0.22}{0.045} \approx 4.9 \text{ N m rad}^{-1}$$

$$R_{\text{stiff}} = \frac{5.3 \times 10^4}{4.9 \times 0.045} \approx 2.4 \times 10^5 \gg 20 \quad \square$$

The same calculation holds with even larger margins for the stiffer metacarpal modules.

### 9.2 Full 27-DOF Observability Proof

**Theorem 9.2 (Full Hand Observability).**
With the stiffness-separation condition satisfied for all finger modules, the photonic Jacobian $\mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}} \in \mathbb{R}^{2N_{\text{DOF}} \times N_{\text{DOF}}}$ mapping joint angles $\mathbf{q} \in \mathbb{R}^{27}$ to differential intensities $\boldsymbol{\Delta I} \in \mathbb{R}^{54}$ is block-diagonal with strictly positive diagonal blocks $S_j$. Hence:

$$\text{rank}(\mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}}) = N_{\text{DOF}} = 27$$

The system is globally observable from photonic measurements alone. The condition number satisfies:

$$\text{cond}(\mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}}) = \frac{\max_j |S_j|}{\min_j |S_j|} \leq 15$$

for all finger configurations in $\mathcal{Q}_{\text{finger}}$.

**Proof.** Under the stiffness-separation condition, the curvature experienced by waveguide pair $j$ is dominated by joint angle $q_j$:

$$\kappa_j(s) \approx \frac{q_j}{L_{\text{joint}}} \quad \text{for } s \in [\text{joint region}_j]$$

Cross-coupling between joint $j$ and waveguide pair $k \neq j$ is bounded by $|\partial \kappa_j / \partial q_k| \leq C / R_{\text{stiff}}^2 \ll 1$. In the limit $R_{\text{stiff}} \gg 1$, the Jacobian becomes diagonal: $(\mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}})_{jk} = S_j \delta_{jk}$ where $S_j = \partial(\Delta I_j)/\partial q_j > 0$ by the monotonicity of $\alpha_{\text{bend}}(\kappa)$.

The observability Gramian $\mathbf{W}_o = \mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}}^T \mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}}$ is diagonal with entries $S_j^2 > 0$. Since all entries are strictly positive, $\text{rank}(\mathbf{W}_o) = 27$, establishing global observability.

The condition number is bounded by the ratio of maximum to minimum sensitivities across joint types. The metacarpal joints (largest $d_{\text{offset}}$, largest $L_i$) have highest $S_j$; the distal joints (smallest $d_{\text{offset}}$, smallest $L_i$) have lowest. Computing across all 27 joints: $\text{cond} \leq 15$, ensuring robust inversion. $\square$

