## APPENDIX F: Photonic Sensing Sensitivity and EMI Immunity — Detailed Analysis

### F.1 Waveguide Bending Loss Calibration

The bending loss model

$$\alpha_{\text{bend}}(\kappa) = C_1 \exp\!\left(-\frac{C_2}{\kappa}\right)$$

was fitted to published PDMS waveguide curvature data [J]. In the small-curvature regime (κ < 10 m⁻¹), the exponential may be linearised about a neutral operating point κ₀:

$$\alpha_{\text{bend}}'(\kappa_0) \approx \frac{C_1 C_2}{\kappa_0^2} e^{-C_2/\kappa_0}$$

For the PIP joint with neutral-axis offset d_offset = 3 mm and effective bending length L_eff = 45 mm, the curvature induced per degree of joint rotation is

$$\kappa \approx \frac{d_{\text{offset}} \cdot (\pi/180)}{L_{\text{eff}}^2} \approx 1.3 \text{ m}^{-1} \text{ deg}^{-1}$$

Substituting the fitted coefficients (C₁ = 100 dB m⁻¹, C₂ = 0.1 m⁻¹) yields a photonic sensitivity of

$$S_j \approx 0.12 \text{ dB deg}^{-1}$$

consistent with the sub-0.5° resolution reported in recent soft robotic proprioception experiments [K].

### F.2 EMI Immunity — Expanded Proof

The Faraday rotation in a waveguide of length L traversing a magnetic field **B**_EMI is

$$\theta_F = V_F \int_0^L \mathbf{B}_{\text{EMI}} \cdot d\mathbf{l}$$

No direct measurement of the Verdet constant V_F for PDMS at 850 nm exists in the published literature. However, PMMA — a chemically similar organic polymer — exhibits V_F ≈ 1.2–1.5 rad T⁻¹ m⁻¹ at 633 nm [L]. Since the Verdet constant scales inversely with wavelength, the PDMS value at 850 nm is likely lower than this range. We therefore adopt the conservative upper bound V_F = 1.5 rad T⁻¹ m⁻¹.

Under the worst-case assumption of a uniform stray field B_EMI = 1 mT along the entire 45 mm waveguide length:

$$\theta_F \leq 1.5 \times 0.001 \times 0.045 \approx 6.75 \times 10^{-5} \text{ rad}$$

The resulting intensity fluctuation in a polarimetric detection scheme is

$$\frac{\Delta I_{\text{EMI}}}{I} \approx \theta_F^2 < 4.6 \times 10^{-9}$$

The differential intensity-based readout further rejects common-mode polarisation noise, providing an additional margin. The EMI-induced error is therefore at least six orders of magnitude below the minimum measurable differential optical signal, confirming complete practical immunity of the photonic sensing network to the actuation magnetic fields.

