## APPENDIX G: Thermal and Power Budget — Detailed Calculation

### G.1 Coil Thermal Model

For a rectangular coil of dimensions w × l with N turns of wire of cross-sectional area A_wire, the coil resistance is

$$R_c = \frac{\rho_{\text{Cu}} N (2w + 2l)}{A_{\text{wire}}}$$

The surface area available for convective cooling, taken as the coil perimeter times the coil stack thickness h_stack ≈ 2 mm, is approximately

$$A_{\text{surf}} \approx 2(w + l) h_{\text{stack}}$$

The steady-state temperature rise at continuous current I is

$$\Delta T = \frac{I^2 R_c}{h_{\text{conv}} A_{\text{surf}}}$$

with natural convection coefficient h_conv = 10 W m⁻² K⁻¹ (still air).

**Numerical evaluation for the proximal phalanx coil.** Parameters: N = 80 turns, w = 5 mm, l = 4 mm, A_wire = 0.05 mm², ρ_Cu = 1.68 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m. This yields:

$$R_c = \frac{1.68\times10^{-8} \times 80 \times (0.010 + 0.008)}{0.05\times10^{-6}} \approx 3.6 \text{ Ω}$$

$$A_{\text{surf}} \approx 2(0.005 + 0.004) \times 0.002 = 3.6 \times 10^{-5} \text{ m}^2$$

At I = 1 A: P_J = 3.6 W, giving ΔT ≈ 100°C in still air without additional heat sinking. This exceeds the 80°C operating limit for the N52 magnet grade. Two mitigation strategies are adopted:

- **Reduced continuous current:** I_cont ≤ 0.7 A, reducing ΔT to approximately 49°C.
- **Integrated micro heat-spreader:** A 50 µm copper foil liner bonded to the inner CFRP shell wall. COMSOL coupled electromagnetic-thermal simulations (Section 13.1) confirm that with this foil in place, T_coil < 80°C at I = 1 A continuous operation.

### G.2 Full-Hand Power Budget

With N_a = 54 actuator coils (two per active joint), the peak instantaneous electrical power if all coils were simultaneously driven at I = 1 A would be:

$$P_{\text{peak}} = 54 \times 3.6 = 194 \text{ W}$$

Dexterous manipulation tasks involve a simultaneous activation duty cycle of approximately 30% and an average per-coil current of 0.7 A. The average system actuation power is therefore:

$$P_{\text{act,avg}} \approx 0.3 \times 54 \times (0.7^2 \times 3.6) \approx 28.6 \text{ W}$$

Adding the photonic sensing network (148 channels × 5 mW = 0.74 W) and on-board control electronics (approximately 5 W):

$$P_{\text{total,avg}} \approx 28.6 + 0.74 + 5 \approx 34.3 \text{ W}$$

With the allocated 20 Wh Li-Po battery (200 g), continuous manipulation endurance is approximately 35 minutes. For intermittent grasping with average power below 10 W, endurance exceeds 2 hours. Both figures satisfy the design targets stated in Section 10.

### G.3 Actuator Force-to-Mass Ratio

The system force-to-mass ratio for a single coil module, including the coil, the Halbach ring pair, and the mechanical support structure, is:

$$\Gamma_{\text{sys}} = \frac{F_L}{m_{\text{coil}} + m_{\text{Halbach}} + m_{\text{support}}} = \frac{2NIwB_0}{m_{\text{coil}} + m_{\text{Halbach}} + m_{\text{support}}}$$

For the proximal phalanx coil (F_L = 0.64 N at I = 1 A) with component masses: coil ≈ 1.5 g, Halbach ring pair ≈ 2.0 g, support structure ≈ 0.5 g, total m_act = 4.0 g:

$$\Gamma_{\text{sys}} = \frac{0.64}{0.004} = 160 \text{ N kg}^{-1}$$

For the metacarpal coil operating at B₀ = 0.9 T with larger wire gauge, Γ_sys approaches 180 N kg⁻¹. These values are characteristic of high-quality voice-coil actuators and confirm the 80–180 N kg⁻¹ range asserted in the main text.

