## APPENDIX H: Fabrication Feasibility and Manufacturing Roadmap

The PCMDHA's manufacturing challenges are explicitly acknowledged in Section 12.5. This appendix outlines the proposed fabrication sequence and maps each step to current technology readiness levels.

### H.1 Phalanx Shell Fabrication

**Continuous-fibre additive manufacturing (CFAM).** Multi-axis robotic placement of pre-impregnated carbon fibre tows onto a sacrificial mandrel produces the hollow, thin-walled shell (t_c = 0.3–0.6 mm) with internal strut anchor points integrated into the layup. This approach is in active industrial use for aerospace structures (Markforged continuous fibre, 9T Labs Red Series).

**Mandrel removal.** Achieving the η* ≈ 0.94 hollowness ratio requires removal of the inner core after curing. Soluble polymer mandrels (e.g., PVA) or low-melting-point alloy cores (e.g., Field's metal, T_melt = 62°C) are both compatible with CFRP cure temperatures.

### H.2 Embedding of Waveguides and Micro-Coils

**Waveguide integration.** After shell curing, PDMS waveguide cores (300 µm diameter) are deposited in situ using five-axis extrusion-based direct-write printing. A first pass deposits the fluorinated polymer cladding channel; a second pass fills the channel with uncured PDMS under vacuum, followed by thermal cure at 60°C. This process is an extension of published direct-write polymer waveguide techniques.

**Coil integration.** Micro-coils of 50–80 turns are wound on 3D-printed polymer formers matched to the inner phalanx lumen geometry. An automated needle-winding head guided by the five-axis platform inserts and bonds each former inside the hollow shell before the distal Halbach ring is attached.

### H.3 Halbach Ring Assembly

**Segment magnetisation and alignment.** NdFeB ring segments are laser-cut from bulk sintered magnet blocks and assembled onto precision-machined CFRP joint-interface rings using custom magnetisation jigs that enforce the required Halbach rotation pattern. Segment positioning accuracy of ±0.1 mm is required to maintain the < 3% field-uniformity target.

**Structural bonding.** Hysol EA 9394 epoxy (shear strength > 20 MPa) bonds the assembled Halbach rings to the CFRP phalanx end-faces. Bond-line thickness is controlled to 0.05 ± 0.01 mm by a film adhesive variant.

### H.4 Technology Readiness Levels and Path Forward

| TRL | Description | Status |
|---|---|---|
| TRL 1–2 | Analytical models and multiphysics simulations | Completed (this paper) |
| TRL 3 | Single phalanx prototype: holding force, bending-loss sensitivity, thermal steady state | Near-term; pending funding |
| TRL 4–5 | Three-phalanx finger assembly: kinematic workspace verification, photonic crosstalk characterisation | Follow-on experimental paper |
| TRL 6 | Full five-finger hand in controlled laboratory environment | 3–5 year horizon |

All processes draw on commercially available equipment and materials. The primary fabrication challenge is the simultaneous integration of 148 waveguides, 54 micro-coil actuators, and 27 Halbach ring pairs within a total hand mass budget of 360 g. The authors assess this as feasible within the next 3–5 years given the rapid advancement of multi-material additive manufacturing and automated precision assembly. A companion experimental paper will document the TRL 3 demonstration.

